# examples/dungeon/: a 64x64 dungeon chunk grown from one 64-bit seed, for Kiln,
# driven by its probatio.exsc through every entry point of both modules:
# furor_petabytorum.exsc (the seed: semina_furore, its inverse desemina_furore
# and the mixer under both) and dungeon.exsc (the chunk).
#
# expected.out (17,840 bytes) is WRITTEN BY prototypes/dungeon_oracle.py, not by
# this program: Python's arbitrary-precision integers, list-of-lists grids and
# zlib's crc32. Regenerate it with
#   python3 prototypes/dungeon_oracle.py stream -o tests/programs/dungeon/expected.out
# (about 10 s: the oracle is the slow side). The stream's six sections are
# described in the oracle's source, `WORLDS`' comment: the petabyte arithmetic,
# the seed function, twenty-two chunk records (the first four followed by their raw
# 4,096 bytes), a rolled digest over 256 chunks, the prune run on a grid
# built to have islands in it, and the seed's inverse (22 words).
#
# The exit status is the program's own check of what is NOT the oracle's word: 0
# only if the mixer reproduces splitmix64's published first output for seed 0
# (0xE220A8397B1DCDAF), the zero seed makes the same chunk as the constant that
# replaces it, the prune removes exactly the 11 stranded tiles of its synthetic
# grid and refuses a start that is not floor, the petabyte arithmetic holds, and
# `desemina_furore` gives back the index `semina_furore` was handed for every
# (world, index) of the scenario; otherwise 1, 2, 3, 4 or 5 in that order.
#
# NON-VACUOUS, measured 2026-10-01: of twenty-five mechanical mutants of
# the two modules, every one fails here, each at a stated place --
#   roughen rate 1/4 -> 1/8              byte 200   (the first chunk's draw count)
#   CA wall rule >= 5 -> >= 6            byte 200
#   CA floor rule <= 3 -> <= 2           byte 200
#   corridor order bit inverted          byte 200
#   BSP split third -> quarter           byte 200
#   xorshift's third shift 17 -> 16      byte 200
#   draw counter += 2                    byte 207   (the draws word, and only it)
#   door "open area" <= 3 -> <= 4        byte 229   (a chunk's CRC)
#   CRC polynomial's low bit             byte 229
#   trap interior test == 0 -> <= 1      byte 4373  (chunk 5; traps are rare)
#   mixer constant off by one            exit 1, byte 49
#   multiply carry shifted               exit 1, byte 49
#   seed ignores the world               exit 5, byte 113   (the seed section)
#   seed stride GAMMA off by one         exit 5, byte 49
#   inverse: GAMMA's inverse, either mixer constant's inverse, the xorshift
#     order, or the world's sign wrong   exit 5, byte 17,673 or 17,737 (section F)
#   inverse: one xorshift round, not two exit 5, byte 17,673
#   zero seed not substituted            exit 2, byte 199
#   prune loses its left move           exit 3, byte 199   (over-prunes: any chunk shows it)
#   prune loses its down move            exit 3, byte 199
#   prune also walks diagonals           byte 17,648 -- ONLY the synthetic grid (E)
#   prune call removed from the chunk    byte 17,544 -- ONLY the two prune-firing
#                                        chunk records; the 20 older records are
#                                        byte-identical with it disabled
# The inverse's xorshift loop used to run THREE rounds. Mutation found the third
# redundant -- dropping it survived -- and the code now runs two, which is what
# ceil(64 / k) - 1 gives when r starts at the input; one round is caught.
#
# The prune mutants after those are why sections C's final two records and E exist. The prune fires
# in about one chunk in 7,000 (283 of 2,000,000, tests/c/dungeon_scan.c), so
# twenty seeds drawn at random almost never meet it; the two records are seeds
# where it does (it removes 1 tile in one and 5 in the other), and the synthetic
# grid pins 4-connectivity with an island that touches only at a corner.
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-01 (each phase driven by hand, one program at a time --
# tests/run.sh was not run; this environment's clang has no UBSan runtime and
# no target headers, so its differential and cross phases would fail on every
# program for reasons that are the host's):
#   reference   exit 0, 17,840 bytes, byte-identical to expected.out, ~1.0 s
#   gcc   -O0   exit 0, identical (-fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all)
#   gcc   -O2   exit 0, identical
#   clang -O0   exit 0, identical (-fsanitize=undefined -fsanitize-trap=undefined)
#   clang -O2   exit 0, identical
#   cross       clang --target=mips64-unknown-linux-musl -mabi=n32 -march=mips3,
#               run under qemu-mipsn32: exit 0, identical, ~0.5 s -- the
#               generator's integer arithmetic, its trapping index math and its
#               32-bit `mensura` agree big-endian. The o64 ABI itself is still
#               [UNTESTED], as spec 9.5 says of every cross=yes row.
#   audit       tools/syscall-audit.sh: exit_group, write, read -- nothing else.
#
# What this does NOT measure: time on a VR4300. Nothing here, and nothing in
# the spec's claims about this module, is a cycle count.
TEST: expect-exit=0 sources=examples/dungeon/furor_petabytorum.exsc,examples/dungeon/dungeon.exsc,examples/dungeon/probatio.exsc cross=yes
